sections.body:
The most tempting way to combine a BM25 score and a vector similarity score for the same document is to just add them (or take a weighted sum). This is a real, easy-to-make bug, not a harmless simplification, because the two scores don't live on comparable scales at all. BM25 scores are unbounded and corpus-dependent -- a score of 8.2 in one corpus and query means something entirely different from an 8.2 in another; cosine similarity, by contrast, is always bounded to [-1, 1].

```python
bm25_scores = {"docA": 8.2, "docB": 1.1, "docC": 0.4}
vector_scores = {"docA": 0.62, "docB": 0.95, "docC": 0.58}

naive_combined = {d: bm25_scores[d] + vector_scores[d] for d in bm25_scores}
print(sorted(naive_combined.items(), key=lambda kv: kv[1], reverse=True))
# [('docA', 8.82), ('docB', 2.05), ('docC', 0.98)]
```

docA wins the naive sum decisively -- but look at what actually drove that: docA's BM25 score of 8.2 towers over docB's 1.1, while on the vector side, docB (0.95) is actually the strongest match, clearly ahead of docA (0.62). Because BM25's scale in this corpus happens to be roughly ten times larger than cosine similarity's fixed [-1, 1] range, the sum is almost entirely determined by whichever score has the larger typical magnitude, regardless of which one is actually more informative for this specific query. This is the same family of problem as the raw-dot-product-versus-magnitude issue from earlier in this subject, just occurring between two entirely different retrieval systems instead of between two vectors within one system.

